-You can choose from three simple example V2X applications to run with *socktap* via the `--applications` argument:
+You can choose from seven simple example V2X applications to run with *socktap* via the `--applications` argument:
 
 - *ca* sends Cooperative Awareness Messages (CAM)
 - *hello* sends simple BTP-B message with the binary payload `0xc0ffee`
 - *benchmark* counts the number of any received messages and prints the current message rate once per second
+- *tlm* sends Signal Phase and Timing Extended Messages (SPATEM) of an example intersection
+- *rlt* sends MAP Extended Messages (MAPEM) describing the lanes of this intersection at the station's position
+- *tlc-request* sends Signal Request Extended Messages (SREM) of a tram asking for priority and prints the answers
+- *tlc-status* answers SREM for the example intersection by Signal Request Status Extended Messages (SSEM)
+
+The example intersection joins a main road with tram tracks and a side road: nine signal groups for vehicles, pedestrians and trams, two stages and five coordinated fixed-time plans selected by a weekly schedule (see `vanetza/facilities/fixed_time_plan.hpp`).
+Protected or permissive movement states are derived from conflicting paths of signal groups served in the same stage.
+The example controller has no priority logic, so *tlc-status* answers valid requests with status *processing*.
+For example, run a road-side and a vehicle station on one host:
+
+    :::shell
+    socktap -l udp -a tlm rlt tlc-status --positioning static --latitude 49.0 --longitude 11.4 --intersection-id 7
+    socktap -l udp -a tlm rlt tlc-request --spatem-interval 0 --mapem-interval 0 --intersection-id 7 --mac-address 02:00:00:00:00:02 --print-rx
+
+See [Generating SPATEM and MAPEM](../recipes/spatem-mapem.md) for the underlying helper functions.
 
 
